🧠 History & Philosophy of Data (Narrative, Conceptual)

How Data Happened — Chris Wiggins & Matthew L. Jones
The genealogy of data thinking.
From Enlightenment statistics → bureaucracy → algorithms. Calm, explanatory, intellectually serious.

The Information — James Gleick
A sweeping intellectual history of information itself: signals, codes, entropy, data, networks.
Very much “ideas + movement,” not policy.

A History of Data Visualization and Graphic Communication
Scholarly but readable history of how humans learned to see data.
Excellent for grounding modern visualization intuitions.

📊 Data Visualization (Theory, Aesthetics, Meaning)

The Visual Display of Quantitative Information — Edward Tufte
Canonical.
Not a manual — a philosophy of clarity, honesty, and compression in data.

Envisioning Information
More expaAsive, more visual, more conceptual than the first.
Strongly aligned with interactive intuition.

Cartographies of Time
A visual history of timelines.
Shows how humans spatialize time, causality, and sequence — extremely relevant to data dashboards.

Raw Data Is an Oxymoron
Short essays showing data is always constructed.
Very clean epistemology.